  "sections": [
    {
      "key": "quickFacts",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/09-characoids-tetra-relationship/anodus-orinocensis-2/",
      "html": "<p>Anodus orinocensis was imported from Venezuela for the first time. With a final length of a good 30 cm it becomes considerably larger than Hemiodus gracilis. It is peaceful.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "distribution",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/09-characoids-tetra-relationship/anodus-orinocensis-2/",
      "html": "<p>Anodus orinocensis comes from Venezuela, in the Orinoco.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "waterConditions",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/09-characoids-tetra-relationship/anodus-orinocensis-2/",
      "html": "<p>The water composition is not important, but like so many species from the Orinoco, A. orinocensis needs warmth and should be kept in the range of 24-30°C.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "diet",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/09-characoids-tetra-relationship/anodus-orinocensis-2/",
      "html": "<p>Anodus is an Aufwuchs eater and also filters the water for small organisms. It requires an attentive diet with the smallest possible food (cyclops, fine flake food, small granules).</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "behaviour",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/09-characoids-tetra-relationship/anodus-orinocensis-2/",
      "html": "<p>As a young fish A. orinocensis often lives in mixed shoals with Hemiodus gracilis. The two species look amazingly similar, but Anodus swims mostly in a \"normal\" horizontal position, while H. gracilis is a small animal eater and almost always swims in a slanting head-up position. They are peaceful animals.</p>"
    }
  ],
